WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:06.680
In 1966, on the south eastern coast of Barbados,
the US Army in partnership with the Canadian

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.290
Government, began the earth shuddering tests
of this massive artillery gun.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:18.860
A 36 metre long barrel constructed by welding
two 16 inch naval guns together end to end.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.390
Loaded with over half a metric tonne of black
powder, the barrel needed rings of stiffening

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:28.700
reinforcement along its length to withstand
the immense pressure developed inside.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:33.540
Long white straightening rods needed to carefully
adjust the barrel to shoot straight and true.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.150
[1]
Its white paint gives away its true purpose.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.510
This was no military gun painted in drab olive
camouflage.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.239
This was a scientific instrument.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:44.600
A space cannon.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:50.650
A gun capable of launching a projectile to
an altitude of 180 kilometres.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:55.920
Nearly half way to the orbiting altitude of
the international space station, and the altitude

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:02.039
of the lowest orbiting satellite ever
This gun was undeniably a space gun.

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:06.440
Holding the record of highest altitude projectile
ever launched.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.290
The history of this bizarre science project
is a fascinating story.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:16.330
From the advances in ballistic science needed
to achieve this extraordinary feat, to the

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:22.060
reasons Barbados, a small island in the Caribbean
was chosen for the gun's location.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:41.159
A tale that ends with bureaucratic sabotage
and the assassination of the lead scientist.

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:45.750
The remnants of these experimental guns remain
here on the beaches of Barbados.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:49.540
Laying idle since their final launch in 1966.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.439
Rusted relics of a bygone era, that you can
catch a glimpse of while taking off from runway

00:01:54.439 --> 00:02:01.119
09 of Grantley Adams International Airport
But why here, why on this beach off the southeastern

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:03.560
coast of Barbados?

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:08.580
Project Harp was a joint effort from the Canadian
Government, funding research at McGill University

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.819
and the US Army’s Ballistics Research Laboratory,
who were seeking more data on upper atmosphere

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:20.860
conditions, in the same decade the SR-71 made
its debut this data was highly valuable,but

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.170
gathering that data with sounding rockets
was expensive.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:30.120
Canada, not short on wide open spaces, could
have located suitable sites near the university’s

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.440
hometown of Montreal.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.190
However, the dean of McGill, Donald Mordell,
had higher ambitions than just gathering upper

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.580
atmosphere data.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:44.190
A vision of a low cost space launch system,
capable of launching satellites into orbit

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.409
for a fraction of the cost.

00:02:46.409 --> 00:02:49.550
Barbados was selected for these reasons.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.580
The most eastern of all the Caribbean islands.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:57.489
Firing from the south eastern shore at a fixed
firing angle over the sea, there was essentially

00:02:57.489 --> 00:02:59.310
unlimited fallout area.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:04.520
With plenty of space for debris from the launches
to fall into the ocean.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.110
Located along the launch path of Cape Canaveral,
the infrastructure needed to track the launches

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.959
already existed nearby.

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:16.410
And, critically, located just 13 degrees north
of the equator, Barbados would benefit from

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:23.050
the increased rotational speed at the equator
and require lowered launch energies as a result.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:28.220
Barbados also came with the added benefit
of being a region of extreme atmospheric research

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:34.060
interest, the nursery of hurricanes that surge
through the gulf of mexico every year.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.810
Barbados of the 1960s was a low income country,
highly dependent on sugar exports, yet to

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:45.610
make the transition to a relatively high income
tourism country independent from the British

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:46.690
empire.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:51.870
The potential of a burgeoning space launch
industry on the tiny island was met with enthusiasm

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.470
by the country's government.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:58.510
Striking a deal with McGill University
To meet its objective Project Harp needed

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:03.769
an extremely powerful gun, with a delicate
control over the explosive power pushing the

00:04:03.769 --> 00:04:06.489
payload towards space.

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:09.360
Projectile design is the first area of concern.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.000
This graph shows the relationship between
shot weight and muzzle velocity for the harp

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.180
gun system.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.340
A lower mass projectile will have a higher
muzzle velocity.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:23.660
We need to balance this with the ballistic
coefficient, which is a measure of the projectile's

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.750
ability to resist air resistance and maintain
its velocity.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:32.130
Found by dividing the mass of the projectile
by the drag coefficient multiplied by the

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.300
cross-sectional area.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:40.140
Creating a narrow and dense projectile, essentially
a dart, can maximize ballistic coefficient.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.449
This is why Project Harp utilized sabot rounds.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:48.380
A saboted round is much narrower than the
gun barrel, allowing it to minimize its cross

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.889
section and maximize it’s ballistic coefficient.

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:56.620
However we need a method of creating a seal
with the gun barrel, allowing the propellants

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:58.870
pressure to act on the projectile.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:05.009
There are several design options for sabots,
but as we saw, projectile weight affects muzzle

00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:11.380
velocity, and these sabots increase the effective
projectile weight, despite falling away upon

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:12.789
leaving the barrel.

00:05:12.789 --> 00:05:16.919
So it’s vital to keep these plates as light
as possible.

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:21.360
The main body of a pusher plate is made from
lightweight aluminum, but the aluminum is

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.980
quite soft and the base of the missile has
a low area.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:29.690
With the immense pressure pushing on the fins
from below, they can begin to sink into the

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:30.860
aluminum.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:36.760
To counteract this a high strength steel insert
is often added for the projectile to sit upon.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.760
We then need some method of keeping the projectile
aligned in the barrel.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:46.230
This often was as simple as placing laminated
plywood cutouts between each fin.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.960
Finally we need a soft material that won’t
scratch the barrel to form a tight seal between

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:56.290
the sabot and the barrel, for this Project
Harp used polyethylene.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.660
The altitude reached is ultimately decided
by the velocity of the projectile exiting

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.150
the barrel, the muzzle velocity.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:07.620
From here pressure from the guns barrel will
no longer be acting on the projectile and

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.590
atmospheric drag will begin to sap away the
kinetic energy imbued to it.

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.710
Using gunpowder as an energy source is a challenge.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:20.080
Produce too much energy too quickly, and the
barrel of the gun will be over pressured.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.840
At best this damages the gun, at worst it
creates a bomb.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.580
The key to ballistic performance is this graph.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.550
The gun pressure distribution (page 171 of
book).

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:33.710
On the x-axis we have the distance from the
breech face, and on the y-axis we have pressure.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:35.990
Which may look something like this.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:39.870
Over the centuries there have been many ways
of assessing the pressure along points in

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:44.630
the barrel, with some of the earliest being
simple copper or lead plates placed inside

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:48.970
the gun barrel that would deform according
to the pressure they experienced,

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:52.590
but the piezoelectric crystal sensor revolutionised
the science.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:57.490
[2] This sensor, which uses a piezoelectric
crystal, could measure a varying pressure

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:02.800
over time, providing a real time measurement
of pressure, not a single maximum pressure

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.919
point provided by a metal deformation plate.

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:10.680
Piezoelectric means a material, typically
a crystal, generates an electric current when

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.840
mechanical stress is applied, a current which
can be measured to generate a pressure reading.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.949
[3]
These sensors can be placed at the bottom

00:07:17.949 --> 00:07:21.520
of rounds to develop optimised pressure gradients.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.729
Allowing scientists to test new methods.

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:29.020
With the ultimate goal of maximising the area
under this curve, that’s the total energy

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:33.880
released, without exceeding the maximum pressure
the gun can handle.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.580
Project HARP used some clever methods to achieve
this.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:40.310
First, the most obvious, extending the length
of the barrel.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.750
The longer the barrel the more time the pressure
has to act on the projectile.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:49.810
However as the propellant continues to burn,
and the volume behind the projectile increases,

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:53.120
the pressure behind it continually diminishes.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:58.300
Eventually aerodynamic drag and friction between
the projectile and the barrel will equal the

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:02.930
pressure behind the projectile and no further
gains in velocity are possible.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:07.490
So we need to figure out what the optimal
barrel length for a given charge and projectile

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:08.729
will be.

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:12.900
Next we need to maximise the energy released
from our propellant.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.900
To increase muzzle velocity Project Harp needed
black powder, A LOT of black powder.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:23.310
They used huge 50 kilogram propellant bags
that were rammed into the barrel with a hydraulic

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:25.400
press behind the projectile.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.030
Stacked directly on top of each other, each
having ignition charges sewn into the back

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.940
of each bag facing the primer.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:37.770
These ignition charges only worked when the
initial flame front from the primer reached

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.310
them with sufficient temperatures.

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:44.680
Finally a large air gap between the final
bag and the sabot base was included.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:49.720
But tests using this configuration did not
end as expected.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:54.400
This is a typical pressure-time diagram output
by the pressure sensors inside the barrels

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.110
for these early tests.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:00.660
The peak pressure here is a lot higher than
the scientists predicted and the muzzle velocity

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:02.890
also came in lower than expected.

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:08.360
Clues to the cause began to emerge upon inspecting
the polyethylene disk from the bottom of the

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:09.579
sabot.

00:09:09.579 --> 00:09:14.390
Grains of unburnt black powder were lodged
in the soft white plastic.

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:19.140
Unburnt black powder means the total energy
potential of the propellant was not released,

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.850
but also indicates a larger problem.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:24.590
This is what was theorised to be happening.

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:29.240
The primer ignites the first black powder
bag, creating a pressure front which forces

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:34.240
the column of bags towards the sabot base,
while hot gas flows around the edges towards

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.769
the projectile.

00:09:35.769 --> 00:09:40.350
The flame front begins marching forward at
a slower rate than the pressure front, while

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:45.350
the pressure front compresses the bags preventing
further gas from flowing around them, impeding

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.290
the flame front further.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:52.660
The column impacts the sabot at high speed,
the force of this impact was at times large

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.519
enough to cause damage to the sabot and projectile.

00:09:56.519 --> 00:10:01.959
At this time the flame front catches up, and
a high pressure region is created in the compacted

00:10:01.959 --> 00:10:07.839
zone, which travels backwards towards the
breech, wasting precious kinetic energy.

00:10:07.839 --> 00:10:14.399
Testing new methods with the only 16 inch
120 foot long gun in the world was not feasible,

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:17.890
so interior ballistics tests were performed
with this.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:23.700
A massive instrumented barrel on rails that
recoil backwards down the rails with each

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:28.769
firing, allowing the researchers to develop
new ignition methods in relative safety.

00:10:28.769 --> 00:10:33.060
Although it was called the HARP Flyer because
it would frequently fly off its mountings

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:35.250
when tests overpressured it.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:40.519
A solution to the black powder bags racing
ahead of the flame front was devised by placing

00:10:40.519 --> 00:10:43.730
light wooden spacers between the bags.

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:48.190
These spacers prevented the movement of the
bags, but also helped the bags further along

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:54.690
the barrel to ignite as hot gas could reach
them in the air gaps provided by the spacers.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:58.860
Using the HARP flyer, ballistic performance
graphs comparing the two methods were created,

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.790
and they looked like this.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:06.450
With peak pressure lowering and the area underneath
the curve being larger for the spaced charges.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:10.930
This did not solve the issue completely and
the final configuration included electrically

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:15.209
fired squids to simultaneously fire all bags
together.

00:11:15.209 --> 00:11:19.610
The projectiles themselves took many forms
with different payloads and designs.

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:25.430
One fascinating payload came in the form of
tri-methyl-aluminium, or TMA, a liquid chemical

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:30.470
that was loaded into the inside of the missile,
but this was not a propellant.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:35.760
The liquid was pumped from the rocket with
a piston driven by compressed nitrogen gas,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.520
triggered by a timer.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:43.269
Once outside the rocket the TMA began to react
with oxygen in the ozone layer, forming a

00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:45.019
luminescent tracer cloud.

00:11:45.019 --> 00:11:47.880
A cloud that glowed in the dark.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.709
Allowing ground observers to easily see it.

00:11:50.709 --> 00:11:55.589
This tracer was used to analyse wind shere
in the upper atmosphere, vital data in an

00:11:55.589 --> 00:11:58.880
era of intense aerospace development.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:03.150
Project Harp provided a cheap and reliable
method of studying the upper atmosphere when

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:08.070
compared to sounding rocket alternatives,
and the US Army’s funding was tied to this

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:12.820
objective, but the Canadian leadership of
Project Harp had loftier ambitions,

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:17.000
centred around a massive gun capable of launching
satellites to space.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:23.100
A gun with twice the bore diameter of Project
Harps largest gun, at 32 inches, with thick

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.900
solid steel walls to withstand the expected
increase in barrel pressure.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:32.829
However the real challenge would be in the
development of a multistage, high mass fraction

00:12:32.829 --> 00:12:40.160
rocket system capable of firing from the gun
and accurately placing a satellite into orbit.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.810
The Martlet 4 was designed for this purpose.

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:48.440
A full bore design, removing the need for
a sabot, only the polyethylene disk was included

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:52.550
to protect the bottom of the rocket from the
hot gas inside the barrel.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:54.570
This is a massive challenge.

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:58.200
During launch the rocket would be under extreme
compressive load.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:02.750
This would cause the rocket to bulge outwards
creating friction with the barrel.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:07.810
On one test the fibreglass rocket actually
got stuck inside the barrel and the gas pressure

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:10.220
burst
through the middle of the rocket blowing the

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:15.110
internals of the rocket out of the muzzle
like a giant buckshot cannon.

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:20.029
Developing the system required numerous tests
with inert propellant stand ins.

00:13:20.029 --> 00:13:24.399
Longitudinal stripes were painted onto test
projectiles to examine friction wear after

00:13:24.399 --> 00:13:25.660
each shot.

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:30.530
For these tests the gun was moved to an ice
and snow covered lake north of McGills Montreal

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:31.740
Campus.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:36.000
Here they could fire the projectiles in a
horizontal position, landing on the snow and

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.699
ice and slowly decelerating to be recovered
by the researchers for analysis.

00:13:40.699 --> 00:13:46.380
Another design, the Martlet 3C was designed
to be slightly smaller than the gun bore,

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:50.410
and the space between the barrel and the rocket
casing was filled with a dense fluid that

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:54.779
would travel with the rocket in bore and prevent
friction on the rocket casing.

00:13:54.779 --> 00:13:59.259
It was ultimately decided after testing that
a full bore rocket made from fibreglass with

00:13:59.259 --> 00:14:01.860
a teflon coating was the best option.

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:06.020
When it came time to test active propellant
the propellant grain layers were laminated

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:09.580
together in a hydraulic press around a star
shaped mandrel.

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:14.620
When dried an inhibitor coating was applied,
the end casings, including the nozzle were

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:20.450
fitted and the fibreglass casing was simply
wrapped around the solid propellant and cured.

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:23.180
This was an extremely cheap method of construction.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:25.630
Structural issues were assessed with this
method

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:30.380
and when completed flip out stabilisation
fins were incorporated and the single stage

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.160
Marlet 3E was born.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:38.290
Capable of placing 25 kilogram payload to
an apogee of 450 kilometres.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:41.139
Above the orbiting altitude of the ISS.

00:14:41.139 --> 00:14:46.680
However this was not orbit, without the necessary
horizontal velocity these payloads would simply

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.149
fall back to earth.

00:14:48.149 --> 00:14:51.029
To achieve orbit a multistage system was needed.

00:14:51.029 --> 00:14:52.370
The Marlet 4.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:55.710
However scaling a system like this up would
be extremely difficult.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:59.089
G hardened guidance and control systems needed
to be developed

00:14:59.089 --> 00:15:02.560
Feeding into a central computer that controlled
cold gas thrusters.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:08.291
High acceleration tests proved these systems
were capable of surviving up to 5000 gs, well

00:15:08.291 --> 00:15:10.530
above the launch acceleration.

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:14.940
The staging would necessitate gradual decreasing
of diameter, and so the previous method of

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.170
fluid support was revisited.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:21.140
This optimised staging method was proving
difficult to develop with the time pressure

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:22.600
on the program.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:28.199
The joint US-Canadian program was time limited
to 3 years, and the engineers at project harp

00:15:28.199 --> 00:15:33.709
pursued unguided multistage rockets based
on earlier saboted systems.

00:15:33.709 --> 00:15:39.370
Ultimately the program ended, on June 30th,
1967, before its ambitions could be realized.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:44.670
Managing the bureaucracy between 3 institutions,
a university, a government and a military

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.209
proved too difficult.

00:15:46.209 --> 00:15:48.310
Each having their own priorities.

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:53.440
Leaving the chief engineer behind Project
HARP Gerald Bull, lost in the wind.

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.579
Seeking a new patron from this expertise he
wrote his own demise.

00:15:57.579 --> 00:16:01.579
Beginning by selling and smuggling weapons
to the South African government, ignoring

00:16:01.579 --> 00:16:06.570
a United Nations arms embargo and earning
himself 6 months in US prison.

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:10.600
Upon release he continued selling arms to
the South African government and got caught

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:11.800
and fined again.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:14.830
Eventually moving to Brussels to ply his trade
there.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:18.740
[4]
In 1981 he was approached by the Iraqi government,

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:21.060
led by the now infamous Saddem Hussain.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:26.399
With a 25 million dollar paycheck, Bull took
the knowledge he gained during project harp

00:16:26.399 --> 00:16:30.089
to Iraq to begin a new project, Project Babylon.

00:16:30.089 --> 00:16:34.900
A project with the ultimate goal of building
an artillery weapon capable of firing from

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:37.140
Iraq into Israel and Iran.

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:41.600
A hare-brained scheme, that was proven to
be completely tactically useless during World

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:46.040
War 2, and would be even less effective in
the era of guided missiles.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:51.440
Bull was supposedly aware of how ineffective
this would be as a weapon and used this as

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:56.310
justification to continue his research, but
the Israeli government didn’t see things

00:16:56.310 --> 00:17:01.430
that way and Mr Bull was assassinated as he
placed the key into the door of his Brussels

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:02.430
home.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:07.169
He was found by police lying on the ground
with a briefcase containing 20 thousand dollars

00:17:07.169 --> 00:17:08.169
inside.

00:17:08.169 --> 00:17:13.199
Other projects like Project SHARP, an even
bigger light gas powered gun was tested in

00:17:13.199 --> 00:17:15.400
California in the 90s.

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:20.339
No projectile can exceed the velocity of the
propellant pushing it, and Project SHARP realised

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:24.350
this and began using light weight hydrogen
gas instead.

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:31.360
Capable of achieving a muzzle velocity of
7 km/s, but the estimated 1 billion dollar

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:36.340
funding needed to scale it up never arrived,
and the system ultimately was used to test

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:39.240
subscale hypersonic scramjet designs.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.840
[5]
Today Spinlaunch is attempting to pick up

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:47.370
where Project Harp left off, using a completely
different form of kinetic launch system to

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:51.460
replace large expensive first stage rockets.

